<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Madhya Bharat</h1><p class="st-meta">1948 CE – 1956 CE · capital Gwalior</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The union of Gwalior, Indore and the smaller Malwa states, an Indian state from 1948 to 1956 with Gwalior and Indore as twin capitals.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1948 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> states of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior State</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/indore-state">Indore State</a> joined with more than twenty smaller states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, including Dhar, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dewas-senior">Dewas Senior</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dewas-junior">Dewas Junior</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ratlam-state">Ratlam</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jaora-state">Jaora</a>, to form the United State of Gwalior, Indore and Malwa, known as Madhya <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-india">Bharat</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jivajirao-scindia">Jivajirao Scindia</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior</a> served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yashwant-rao-holkar-ii">Yashwant Rao Holkar II</a> as Uprajpramukh. Gwalior was the winter capital and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/indore-state">Indore</a> the summer capital. It became a Part B state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1950, with an elected government; Indore's textile mills and the rich black-soil farmland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> were its economic base.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the States Reorganisation Act Madhya <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-india">Bharat</a> was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> on 1 November 1956, except Sunel, transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/madhya-bharat.jpg" alt="Madhya Bharat" width="320" height="387"><figcaption>Image: India 1951 Bombay.svg: Superbenjamin this file: Furfur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madhya_Bharat_in_India_(1951).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1948 CE – 1956 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gwalior</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jivajirao Scindia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malvi, Hindi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>United State of Gwalior, Indore and Malwa, Malwa Union</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1952&amp;focus=madhya-bharat" aria-label="Madhya Bharat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6744 1215 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Madhya Bharat lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7064 1471L7018 1386M7064 1471L7064 1471M7064 1471L7029 1595M7064 1471L7032 1586M7064 1471L7032 1586M7064 1471L7015 1599M7064 1471L7003 1563M7064 1471L7003 1574M7064 1471L6991 1610M7064 1471L7008 1620M7064 1471L6995 1593M7064 1471L7114 1550M7064 1471L7043 1549M7064 1471L6998 1568M7064 1471L7006 1549M7064 1471L7038 1548M7064 1471L7074 1513M7064 1471L6996 1606M7064 1471L6985 1604M7064 1471L6998 1564"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1386h0M7064 1471h0M7029 1595h0M7032 1586h0M7032 1586h0M7015 1599h0M7003 1563h0M7003 1574h0M6991 1610h0M7008 1620h0M6995 1593h0M7114 1550h0M7043 1549h0M6998 1568h0M7006 1549h0M7038 1548h0M7074 1513h0M6996 1606h0M6985 1604h0M6998 1564h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7064 1471h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7064 1471h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1952&amp;focus